WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.220
Hello and welcome to the Semantic Interoperability Roadmap Introduction.

2
00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.180
We want to talk about a challenge that touches many organisations.

3
00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:13.840
This is part of an academic research study at Cranfield University.

4
00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:17.180
This research is grounded in manufacturing and engineering.

5
00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:20.720
However, similar challenges show up in other industries.

6
00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:26.320
We want to test whether this framework transfers beyond the original context.

7
00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:31.680
Most organisations run on a patchwork of discrete systems that don't share a common language.

8
00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:37.680
When that happens, users become the glue, manually reconciling data across systems.

9
00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.460
Systems that were never designed to share meaning.

10
00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:45.440
As organisations grow, this patchwork tends to get worse.

11
00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.200
Point-to-point integrations multiply, often custom-built and fragile.

12
00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:55.820
Governance often lags behind and the most common consequence is a gap in time to value.

13
00:00:55.820 --> 00:01:01.300
Organisations can have the technology in place for years before they see any return.

14
00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:06.580
Our proposal is to introduce a semantic layer, a shared machine-readable model of what things

15
00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:08.560
mean across these systems.

16
00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:14.140
By building an ontology, we are translating all the dialects in one shared language, once,

17
00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:18.540
rather than translating it fresh every single time.

18
00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:23.140
So before we get into how, let's define what we are talking about.

19
00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:26.900
Semantic interoperability is the ability of different information systems to share

20
00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:30.460
data with clear and unmistakable meaning.

21
00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:32.740
This is the goal.

22
00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:38.700
An ontology is a shared machine-readable map of what things mean and how they relate.

23
00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:39.840
Take this example.

24
00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:45.420
With an ontology, each of these identifiers map to a single shared concept and defines

25
00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:50.460
the relationships between the concepts, so a query means the same thing no matter which

26
00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:54.380
system it came from.

27
00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:58.460
Every organisation is somewhere on a journey towards this kind of maturity.

28
00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:04.720
From ad hoc and person-dependent to practices being defined and documented, eventually interoperability

29
00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:07.280
is built in by design.

30
00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:14.860
Most organisations are somewhere on this scale, often without a clear sense of exactly where.

31
00:02:14.860 --> 00:02:17.260
This is where we would like your input.

32
00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:21.460
Based on your own experience, where is your organisation today and where you would like

33
00:02:21.460 --> 00:02:25.700
it to be in the short and long term?

34
00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:30.740
We have created a self-assessment with questions across five dimensions.

35
00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:35.140
You will get your current score and some immediate actions towards your short-term goals.

36
00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:40.060
At the end, there are some feedback questions to help validate our research.

37
00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:45.260
If you found this useful and think others could benefit, please share it.

38
00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:51.100
Your input and theirs will help validate whether this research translates across different industries.

39
00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:52.900
Thank you very much for watching.

